Hal writes:

> Apparently the CHALLENGE is still afoot, with Mike assuming that he "won"
> while I, at least, had perceived the opposite.

Well, perhaps the conversation is still "afoot" (though I have only
skimmed the last few entries since there seems to be little content
anymore), but the CHALLENGE itself has been satisfied.

Perhaps CHALLENGE is an unfortunate word choice; there's no "winner"
(what would s/he get?) -- except that both participants (and hopefully
the list at large) wins through reasoned, civilized discussion.

I thought the whole thread was quite good, except for the arguing
about who said what or who should have said what or who was rude
or who was wrong... 

anyway, for the interested, here are the A.S. rules:


First, the philosophy behind it:  It is not explicitly designed
to increase politeness, though it can be used that way.  The
goal is more general -- to increase the thoughtfulness of posts
by requiring statements to be fleshed out in more detail if
challenged.  The 'enforcement' mechanism is based on the premise
that reputation is important.  There is no formal procedure for
guaranteeing that the rules will be followed, but rule-breakers
are open to public scorn for breaking their word.

The rules are very simple.

1. Membership in the A.S. is open and is granted immediately
   upon public declaration.  There are no procedures for
   being thrown out, though a member can be effectively thrown
   out if s/he breaks the rules (see below), and can resign
   at any time.  A member who has resigned may not rejoin.

2. Members may not issue a CHALLENGE until they have been in the
   A.S. for two weeks.

3. A member may CHALLENGE any post by another member.  This is done
   by announcing the CHALLENGE publicly and asking a specific
   question about the challenged post.

4. The challenged member may refuse the challenge by making a public
   detailed explanation.  This may be done only for one of two reasons:
    A) The challenger has broken A.S. rules in the past.
    B) The CHALLENGE question is not relevant to the post being challenged.

5. The challenged member has one week to respond to the CHALLENGE with
   an essay of at least 400 words (not very long!) responding to the
   question.  This post may be delayed if a brief explanation of
   emergency circumstances is posted, or in accordance with rule 7.

6. The challenger then has one week to respond to this reply with a
   400 word rebuttal to the response.  Under no circumstances may
   this requirement be ignored.  It may be delayed as in rule 5.

7. If a members are unable to post because of requirements of other
   societies, they may delay their essays until their posting
   privileges are regained.

Notes:

1) "But people can make arbitrary decisions about whether a question
    is relevant or another member has followed the rules in the past!"

    That's true, though they must justify that decision in public
    where, if the claims are unreasonable, they are open to public
    shredding, thus damaging their reputation.  Readers are smart
    enough to know who's being reasonable and who isn't.

2) "But if somebody meets the requirements with 400 words of garbage,
    they can force others to work without doing so themselves!"

    Yes, at the cost of looking like an idiot.

3) "But what if they don't care about looking like an idiot?"

    Posting 400-word garbage messages to Extropians is against
    list rules.  Disciplinary action beyond the A.S. will handle
    such sociopathic behavior, if repeated.

4)  "What's my incentive to join?"

    There are many benefits to making people answer direct questions.
    All you have to do is be prepared to commit the same amount of
    effort you are asking of another.

Scenarios:  What to CHALLENGE?  And what happens then?

* A posts:  "B is a ninny.  Nyeah nyeah!"
  B challenges: "I take offense at that.  Please explain specifically
   the reasons behind that accusation."
  A does so: "B's feet are too big, makes spelling errors, ..."
  B responds: "What a small-minded view!  My feet are objects of pride..."

  And B's reputation is salvaged (unless A makes a convincing case that
  B really IS a ninny -- in which case, it is now clear).  Alternate
  responses could include official complaints about flaming.

* C posts: "Why would you want to live forever?  Isn't Heaven desirable?"
  D challenges:  "I believe that your posting violates list policy,
  in the following way.... explain why it does not.
  C does: "Christian Heaven involves beneficial self-transformation,
  embodies boundless expansion, ..."
  D responds: "But you didn't answer the question.  On this list ....

  And, if list policy is defined properly, the case is resolved.  Alternate
  challenges by members with more patience may be interested in demolishing
  the metaphysical underpinnings of C's original post.

* E posts: "Of course, X completely overlooked the potential use of
  Resublimated Thiotimoline in the time-warp device..."
  F challenges:  "Hey, speak english, would you?  What is Resublimated
  Thiotimoline and why is it relevant?"
  E responds with an explanation, doubtless useful to many.
  F now has to think about it, and can respond by expanding on the point
  or in any number of relevant ways.

  Thus techno-babble naturally balances out with explanation.

etc.

derek                A.S. member 3/15

