Subject: Is AI's failure evidence for the existence of souls?

Is AI's Failure Evidence for the Existence of Souls?

It seems to me that David has a valid point, but that the result is
very weak.  The reason for this is that in a Bayesian analysis, as I
understand it, it is normally considered correct to assign equal
probabilities to all unknown components, the sum adding up to one.  So,
if I have a bag of black and white marbles of unknown distribution, my
initial assumption is a 50/50 mix, which is then adjusted as I remove
the marbles one by one.

The trouble with assigning a probability to the existence of a soul is
that it is just one of many, many possibilities, so that the initial
probability that must be assigned is vanishingly small.  For example,
some other possibilities might be that AI fails because Jehova (insert
your favorite deity here) does not want it to succeed, so is
deliberately preventing researchers from finding a simple solution, or
that it is just bad luck, or that aliens from the planet Hoova are
manipulating events in AI research labs, or that negative psychic
forces from a future civilization in which AI has destroyed the earth
are traveling backward in time and preventing it from working, etc.
etc.  (Perhaps Carol could help us here :-)

I am being deliberately facetious here, but it does seem clear that an
indefinite list of possibilities could be constructed, none of which
could be _a priori_ ruled out.

Some of these possibilities may be more likely than others, but it
would be very difficult for me to assign a number to the probability
for the existence of souls.  It seems to me to be somewhat begging the
question to postulate a fairly high _a priori_ probability of souls,
then to point to the failure of AI as evidence of even greater
probability.

On the other hand, if there really is more evidence for the existence
of souls than other possibilities one could come up with, then David's
argument is proportionally stronger.

Does this make sense?

