Date: Mon, 24 May 93 13:41:29 CDT
From: derek@cs.wisc.edu (Derek Zahn)
Subject: Eidetic Imagery and Random-Dot Stereograms

A while ago Tim mentioned a Scientific American article that
described people who could look at one half of a random-dot
stereogram on one day and the other half on the next day, and
mentally fuse them.  I was unable to find the article in question,
but Paul Cisek sent me a reference to the following book:

Julesz, Bela
Foundations of Cyclopean Perception
University of Chicago Press, 1971
ISBN 0-226-41527-9

I was able to find this book.  Very interesting.  Here's a report
on the most relevant points (Note in all of this that I am
simply quoting and interpreting the material in this book --
I have not gone to all the referenced source material and bashed
away at it.  I don't have the time or inclination today):

1. Random-dot stereogram:  the idea is to create two images that
individually look like random black-and-white noise but actually
one image contains a duplicate of some part of the other image,
(perhaps slightly shifted).  when both of these images are viewed
at once, stereo fusion reveals the pattern -- often a geometric
figure that seems to float above the random background.

2. Eidetic memory:  Controversial ability to voluntarily recall
visual images having almost photographic accuracy. (people with
this ability are called 'eidetikers').  Apparently, eidetic memory
cannot be retinal; the eidetiker can project the image onto a blank
wall and inspect it with eye movements (without having the image move
around).

3. A "fair number" of children and a very few adults are eidetikers and
   can retain images for several minutes, usually; occasionally much longer.

4. Some experiments:

  * One part of a 100x100 stereogram was shown to an eideteker's left
    eye for 2 minutes;  after a 10 minute rest, the other half was
    presented to the right eye.  The subject correctly reported an
    inverted T in the image, and remarked that the original image
    had not blurred during the 10-minute period.
  
  * The subject was shown one part of a stereogram for four 3-minute
    periods on one day; then, 24 hours later, was shown the second
    part (as above).  The subject was able to fuse them, and correctly
    reported the presence of a raised square in the image.  Further,
    the subject was able to accurately point out the location of the
    corners of the square and correctly indicate its depth.

5. This indicates that eidetic imagery is represented in the nervous
   system before stereopsis.

6. In another experiment, the subject's head was turned 90 degrees
   while forming the eidetic image; this did not affect the ability
   to fuse the images later.

7. The subject was able to retain four 100x100 random dot stereogram
   images simultaneously, recalling the correct one when needed.

8. Often, once the eidetic recalled image appears, it can be held
   for only a few seconds.

9. These abilities exist in a broad range of degrees.  One researcher
   has developed a simple random-dot correlogram that is used to
   screen potential eidetikers.  Presumably, if you are one, you
   know it -- but I could be convinced to scan in that screening
   test and send out a postscript version if there's enough interest.

derek
