I wrote:

 Fifth, the same argument would apply to any other beliefs that this
 community shared, like being more liberal than average, liking Chinese
 food and MIT, etc.

David Friedman responded:

 I was assuming that AI people were (or might be) correctly analysing the
 logic of the situation, making a prediction from a set of assumptions--one
 of which was that mind is a certain sort of thing. The predictions were
 repeatedly falsified. That lowers the probability we should assign to all
 of the assumptions (assuming there is none that we now know with certainty
 to be false and necessary to the prediction). The assumption that mind is
 mechanism was not, so far as I know, a basis for AI people liking Chinese
 food.

Hmm.. So we have this situation where a few early AI folk predicted
too high a rate of progress for their field.  If we don't try to
distinguish which of these folk's shared beliefs were more
responsible, then this mis-prediction is evidence against all of their
shared beliefs.  Similarly, if we don't distinguish which of their
predictions are more or less related to which beliefs, then all of
their predictions should be similarly treated, and AI folks early
support of time-sharing and many advanced programming language
features is in their favor.  

If we start to make distinctions, like assuming that these
over-predictions of progress depended more on a "mechanistic approach
to consciousness" than liking Chinese food, then we can focus blame
more.  Similarly, we might want to conclude that their taste in
programming languages was not much related to their approach to
consciousness.

But before we go to far down this road I'd want to see a little more
evidence.  I'm still not clear on what a non-mechanistic consciousness
is.  And I see only a few failed predictions, but not much repitition,
easily explained as part of a general can-do attitude shared by lots
of computer folks back then.

Robin Hanson


