Subject: Flames, arbitration

  I'm probably letting myself in for a world of nastiness, but here goes.
  I've posted before about flaming, or rather, the reactions to flaming.
Upon reflection, I realize that I haven't made my stance clear.
  I think flaming is the most significant communicating that happens across
e-mail. The technical abstracts and theories are generally recapitulations
of material available at the library. The news items shared were on TV or
available at the newsstand. And although some original ideas are presented,
they are always defended by citing the works of others.
  This is not a complaint or an accusation, just an observation. And I do
not mean to denigrate in any way the service rendered to us all by those
who share reviews of books we wouldn't have come across on our own, or who
give summaries of news items we might have missed, or who give exhaustively
technical explanations of subjects from other disciplines than our own.
  But, the only thing new about these services is the medium itself. it's
essentially a Sophomore bull session.

***** a short apparent digression leading to my point *******

  Today, people lie. They probably lie more than ever before.
  In any face to face conversation, we spend half our time covering up how
we really feel, and the other half trying to read between the lines.
  We listen to what a public figure is saying, while translating what they
really mean.
  We lie to get jobs (Nah, I don't mind the commute. Dress code? No problem).
  We lie to keep jobs (No, Idon't mind giving a urine sample).
  Let us not speculate on sex in the 90's.
  Talk to a marriage counselor some time (socially) and you will find that the
major problem in marriages/relationships today is communications. Conversation
is happening but nothing important is said. And both people know it and 
neither knows what to do about it. Lying is as ingrained as toilet training.
  By and large, nice people don't even have opinions. In conversation they
withhold commitment until they pick up the consensus opinion, then they agree
with that. Remember, these are nice people......the ones that don't know how
to handle aggression (theirs or anyone else's). With reflexes and behaviors
shaped by parents, Dr. Spock, kindergarten, Mr. Rogers, Sesame St., and that
Boot Camp for the superego: High School.
 
****** my long awaited point *********

  IMHO e-mail is the Frontier. The medium is shaping the way we communicate.
Who taught you what :-) meant? It was invented spontaneously because we can't
grin ironically, or wink, or use voice tone to let others no we are joking.
Hell, we can't even hold our hands out to show "No weapons".
  The medium also encourages us to ignore our "toilet training" and actually
speak our minds. Swing wild. Tell the truth as we see it. Or maybe just act 
as nasty as we really are.
  This is the Frontier, let's see how it evolves. Let e-mail grow in it's own
way. Let's not force Robert's Rules on it. I'd rather see customs and
conventions evolve than have laws and rules imposed.
  And finally, I've always found the flaming to be infinitely more entertaining
than the dozen or so rejoinders, calls for arbitration, tsk tsking (my own 
included), and desperate attempts to prove it wasn't a *flame* but a reasoned
argument couched in provocative terms.
  On the Frontier, if you're flamed you can:
a) Flame back
b) Ignore it
But you don't sue them for slander, get a restraining order or go crying to
Daddy.

                                               Mark W. McFadden

P.S. Never trust anyone who volunteers to be an authority. Like Posting Tzar.
     
P.P.S. Harry, you provide a service. I always felt you were drafted into
arbitration.

